Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** (the current stable line at the time of writing).

* It loads **40 SVG icons** that live on your own domain (`https://my‑site.com/icons/…`).  
* It registers each SVG with `map.addImage()` **once the style has finished loading**.  
* It adds a GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) that contains ~5 000 point features, each with a `category` property that matches one of the icon file names (e.g. `"cafe"`, `"park"` …).  
* The symbol layer uses that property to pick the right icon (`icon-image: ['get', 'category']`).  
* Collision handling is left at the default (`icon‑allow‑overlap: false`) so icons that would overlap are hidden.  
* A tiny helper (`loadSvgIcon`) rasterises the SVG into an `HTMLImageElement` (the only way `addImage` can accept an SVG).  
* A fallback (`styleimagemissing`) logs any icon that you forgot to register.

---

## 1️⃣ HTML – the page skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="app.js"></script>   <!-- the code shown in the next section -->
</body>
</html>
```

> **Why the CDN version?**  
> It guarantees you are using the exact v6.x release (`6.11.2` at the time of writing).  
> If you bundle MapLibre yourself, just import the same version.

---

## 2️⃣ JavaScript – `app.js`

```js
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   CONFIGURATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const MAPBOX_TOKEN = '';               // not needed for pure MapLibre tiles
const MAP_STYLE   = 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json'; // any style you like
const ICON_BASE   = 'https://my-site.com/icons/'; // trailing slash!
const ICONS = [
  // list every category you have – the file name (without extension) must match the
  // `category` property in the GeoJSON.
  'cafe', 'park', 'library', 'restaurant', 'school',
  'hospital', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'gym', 'hotel',
  // … add the rest (≈40 total) …
];
const GEOJSON_URL = 'https://my-site.com/data/venues.geojson';

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   MAP INITIALISATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: MAP_STYLE,
  center: [-122.42, 37.77], // San Francisco – change to whatever you need
  zoom: 12,
  // If you use a custom tile source you may need to set `hash: true` or `maxZoom` etc.
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   HELPER – load an SVG, rasterise it, and return the Image element
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvgIcon(url, size = 24) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image(size, size);
    // Important for cross‑origin SVGs – otherwise `addImage` throws.
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load SVG ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   STEP 1 – Register every SVG as a MapLibre image
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  for (const name of ICONS) {
    // Skip if the style already ships an image with that name
    if (map.hasImage(name)) continue;

    const url = `${ICON_BASE}${name}.svg`;
    try {
      const img = await loadSvgIcon(url, 24); // 24 px square – adjust if you like
      // `addImage` options are optional; we keep the defaults (pixelRatio: 1, sdf: false)
      map.addImage(name, img);
      // console.log(`✅ added ${name}`);
    } catch (err) {
      console.warn(err.message);
    }
  }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   STEP 2 – Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 points)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function addVenueSource() {
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: GEOJSON_URL,
    // If you want to fetch the data lazily you could use `data: null` and later
    // `map.getSource('venues').setData(...)`.
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   STEP 3 – Add the symbol layer that draws the icons
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function addVenueLayer() {
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The `icon-image` expression pulls the exact icon name from the feature property.
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],   // e.g. "cafe", "park", …
      // The defaults already hide colliding icons:
      //   'icon-allow-overlap': false,
      //   'icon-ignore-placement': false,
      // If you want *some* icons to stay visible even when they collide,
      // you could enable `icon-optional: true` (only works when a text field is present).
      // For a large dataset you may also want to limit the zoom range where icons appear:
      //   'minzoom': 12,
      //   'maxzoom': 18,
    },
    paint: {
      // Optional: give the icons a subtle halo so they stand out on busy basemaps.
      // This works only on SDF icons; our SVGs are rasterised, so the halo does nothing.
      // If you need a halo, pre‑process the SVGs into SDF (see maplibre‑cartography skill).
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   STEP 4 – Wire everything together
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  // 1️⃣ Register all SVG icons
  await registerIcons();

  // 2️⃣ Add the source & layer
  addVenueSource();
  addVenueLayer();

  // 3️⃣ Optional: listen for missing‑image warnings – helps you spot typos.
  map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
    console.warn(`⚠️  styleimagemissing – "${e.id}" not found in any sprite or runtime image`);
  });
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   OPTIONAL – If you ever need to add a new icon after the map is
   already running (e.g. a user‑uploaded SVG), just call the same
   helper and `map.addImage()` again.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function addCustomIcon(name, svgUrl) {
  if (map.hasImage(name)) {
    console.info(`Image "${name}" already exists – skipping`);
    return;
  }
  const img = await loadSvgIcon(svgUrl, 24);
  map.addImage(name, img);
}
```

---

## How the pieces fit together

| Piece | What it does | Why it matters for your use‑case |
|------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| **`loadSvgIcon()`** | Loads an SVG via an `Image` element, rasterising it to a bitmap that `addImage` can accept. | `map.loadImage()` only supports PNG/WebP/JPEG; SVGs must be turned into an `HTMLImageElement`. |
| **`registerIcons()`** | Loops over the 40 category names, checks `map.hasImage()` (so we don’t duplicate an existing sprite image), then registers each SVG with `map.addImage()`. | Guarantees every possible `category` value has a matching runtime image before the layer is drawn. |
| **`icon-image: ['get', 'category']`** | Expression that reads the `category` property from each feature and uses it as the image ID. | One layer draws *all* 5 000 points, no need for 5 000 separate layers. |
| **Collision handling** (default) | `icon-allow-overlap: false` (the default) + `icon-ignore-placement: false` hide icons that would overlap. | Prevents the “piling‑up” you asked for when zoomed out. |
| **`styleimagemissing` listener** | Logs any icon ID that the style tried to use but wasn’t registered. | Great for debugging typos in the `category` field or missing SVG files. |
| **`addSource` + `addLayer`** | Adds the GeoJSON source and the symbol layer after the icons are ready. | Guarantees the layer can resolve every `icon-image` reference immediately. |

---

## Tweaking the appearance (optional)

* **Size** – If you want larger or smaller icons, change the `size` argument in `loadSvgIcon(url, size)`.  
* **Pixel‑ratio** – For Hi‑DPI screens you can add the `pixelRatio: 2` option to `addImage`.  
* **Halo / color** – Only works on **SDF** images (`sdf: true`). If you need a halo, pre‑process the SVGs into SDF (see the *maplibre‑cartography* skill).  
* **Zoom‑based visibility** – Add `minzoom` / `maxzoom` in the layer’s `layout` to hide icons at very low zoom levels.  
* **Text labels** – If you also want a label (e.g. the venue name) you can add a `text-field` expression and enable `icon-optional: true` so the label stays visible when the icon is culled.

---

## Full‑stack checklist

1. **Host the SVGs** on a domain that serves them with **CORS headers** (`Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` or your site’s origin).  
2. **Make sure the GeoJSON** (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property whose value exactly matches one of the SVG file names (case‑sensitive).  
3. **Deploy the HTML/JS** (the snippet above) on the same origin or any origin that can reach the SVGs and the GeoJSON (again, CORS must be allowed).  
4. **Test** – open the page, open the browser console, and you should see no “styleimagemissing” warnings. Zoom in/out; icons should disappear when they would overlap.

That’s it! With the code above you have a performant, collision‑aware rendering of 5 000 points, each showing its own SVG‑based category icon, all powered by MapLibre GL JS v6. 🎉

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, rasterised into an `Image` element and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property that matches the SVG file name (e.g. `cafe.svg`, `park.svg`).  
* A single **symbol layer** draws the points.  
* The default collision‑detection (`icon‑allow‑overlap: false`) makes icons disappear when they would overlap, so the map stays readable when you zoom out.  
* An optional `icon‑size` expression scales the icons with zoom – you can tweak the numbers to suit your design.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [-122.42, 37.77],   // San Francisco (change to your area)
  zoom: 12,
  hash: true
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of icon IDs (must match the SVG file names, without .svg)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const ICON_IDS = [
  'cafe', 'park', 'restaurant', 'museum', 'library',
  'school', 'hospital', 'pharmacy', 'bank', 'atm',
  // … add the rest of your 40‑plus categories here …
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an Image element
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvgAsImage(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross‑origin SVGs – otherwise addImage throws
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Load *all* icons and register them with the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  const baseUrl = 'https://YOUR_DOMAIN.com/icons/';   // <-- change this

  // Build an array of promises, one per icon
  const loadPromises = ICON_IDS.map(async (id) => {
    const url = `${baseUrl}${id}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvgAsImage(url);
    // Guard against duplicate registration (e.g. if the style already ships one)
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) {
      // `addImage` options are optional – we keep the defaults (pixelRatio: 1, sdf: false)
      map.addImage(id, img);
    }
  });

  // Wait for every icon to finish loading/registration
  await Promise.all(loadPromises);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  When the style is ready, load icons, then add source + layer
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    await registerIcons();               // ← 40 + SVGs are now known to the style
    console.log('All icons registered');

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------
       6️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 venues)
       ---------------------------------------------------------- */
    map.addSource('venues', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://YOUR_DOMAIN.com/venues.geojson'   // <-- change this
    });

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------
       7️⃣  Symbol layer that uses the icons
       ---------------------------------------------------------- */
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'venue-icons',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'venues',
      // The `category` property must contain a value that matches one of ICON_IDS
      layout: {
        // Use an expression so the icon name comes from the feature property
        'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

        // Optional: make icons a little smaller when zoomed out
        'icon-size': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['zoom'],
          10, 0.5,   // at zoom 10 → 50 % of original size
          14, 1.0    // at zoom 14 → 100 % of original size
        ],

        // Default collision handling (icon‑allow‑overlap: false) already
        // hides icons that would overlap.  You can tweak the behaviour:
        // 'icon-optional': true   // hide only the icon, keep the label (if any)
      },
      paint: {
        // If you ever use SDF icons you could colour them here.
        // For ordinary SVGs this does nothing.
        // 'icon-color': '#ff6600',
        // 'icon-halo-color': '#fff',
        // 'icon-halo-width': 1
      }
    });

    console.log('Venue layer added');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Error while setting up the map:', e);
  }
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Optional: listen for missing‑image warnings (helps debugging)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
  console.warn(`Missing image "${e.id}" – check that the SVG exists and is registered`);
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Loads a base style (any style works). | Gives you a map canvas and a `style` object that will later hold the icons. |
| **2️⃣ List of icon IDs** | An array of the identifiers you’ll use in `icon-image`. | Keeps the code DRY – you only change the list when you add/remove categories. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvgAsImage`** | Creates an `Image` element, sets `crossOrigin='anonymous'`, resolves when the SVG finishes loading. | `map.addImage()` expects an `HTMLImageElement` (or `ImageData`). SVGs must be rasterised first. |
| **4️⃣ `registerIcons`** | Loops over every ID, loads the SVG from your domain, registers it with `map.addImage()`. | All icons become part of the style’s sprite‑like registry, so they can be referenced by `icon-image`. |
| **5️⃣ `map.on('load')`** | After the style is ready we register the icons, then add the GeoJSON source and a symbol layer. | Guarantees the style’s sprite system is ready before we call `addImage`. |
| **6️⃣ Symbol layer** | `icon-image` is an expression that reads the feature’s `category` property. | One layer draws *all* 5 000 points, each picking the correct icon automatically. |
| **Collision handling** | The default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) hides icons that would overlap. | Keeps the map readable when you zoom out; you don’t need extra code. |
| **Optional `icon-size`** | Scales icons with zoom. | Prevents icons from looking huge at low zooms and tiny at high zooms. |
| **`styleimagemissing` listener** | Logs any icon that the style tried to use but wasn’t registered. | Very handy while you’re adding the 40 + icons – you’ll see a warning if a `category` value has no matching SVG. |

---

#### Tweaking & Extending

* **Dynamic loading** – If you don’t want to download *all* 40 + SVGs up‑front, you can use `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver` (v6) to lazily fetch an SVG the first time a category appears. The example above is simpler for a modest number of icons.  
* **SDF icons** – If you need a single‑color, halo‑able icon, convert the SVG to an SDF sprite and register it with `{sdf: true}` in `addImage`. Then `icon-color` and `icon-halo-*` work.  
* **Clustering** – For 5 000 points you may also want a cluster layer (`cluster: true` on the source) and show a single aggregated icon when many venues are close together. The collision‑avoidance already prevents pile‑up, but clustering can further improve performance.  

That’s it – copy the HTML into a file, replace the two `YOUR_DOMAIN.com` placeholders with the real URLs for your SVG icons and GeoJSON, and you’ll have a fully functional map where each venue shows its category icon without piling up when you zoom out. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  

* **The current heuristic (`text‑anchor` missing + `symbol‑placement === "point"`) is not reliable.**  
  * Many ordinary point‑label layers (place‑names, POIs, etc.) also have `symbol‑placement: "point"` and often omit `text‑anchor` (it defaults to `"center"`).  
  * Real shield layers *do* set `text‑anchor` (usually `"center"` or `"left"`/`"right"` for line‑shields) and they also have a handful of **shield‑specific** properties that the current test never looks at.  

* **A more robust “is this a shield?” test should look for the *signature* of a shield layer, not for the absence of a generic property.**  
  * Presence of an **icon image that comes from a shield sprite** (`icon‑image` that matches the shield‑naming convention or that is prefixed with a sprite‑sheet id).  
  * **`icon‑text‑fit`** set to `"both"` (or `"width"`/`"height"`). This is the flag that tells MapLibre the badge should stretch to fit the route number.  
  * **`text‑field`** that references the route reference (`{ref}` or `{ref}`‑related tokens such as `{network}_{ref}`) rather than a generic name field.  
  * Optional but useful: `icon‑text‑fit‑padding`, `icon‑size` that varies with `ref_length`, or a `text‑font` that points at the glyphs used for route numbers.  
  * If you have multiple sprite sheets, the **`icon‑image` may be prefixed** (`my-icons:us-interstate_2`). The prefix itself is not a shield indicator, but the *image name* after the colon is.  

Below is a concrete, production‑ready heuristic you can drop into a style‑audit script, followed by an explanation of each check.

---

## 1. Why the old heuristic fails

| Property | What the old test checks | What many non‑shield layers do |
|----------|--------------------------|--------------------------------|
| `layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | “no explicit anchor → assume shield” | `text-anchor` is optional; if omitted the style spec defaults to `"center"`. Almost every point‑label layer (place names, POIs, city labels) leaves it undefined. |
| `layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | “point‑only placement → shield” | Shield layers **also** use `"point"` for the badge‑only point‑layer (e.g. `road_shield_point`). But many place‑name layers use `"point"` as well. The property alone carries no shield semantics. |

Result: **high false‑positive rate** (plain place‑name layers flagged) and **high false‑negative rate** (shield layers that use a zoom‑functioned `symbol-placement` or that set `text-anchor` explicitly are missed).

---

## 2. What a shield layer *actually* looks like in the style spec

A typical shield definition (OSM Bright / OSM Liberty) contains **all** of the following:

```json
{
  "id": "road_shield_point",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "road_2",               // or "my-icons:us-interstate_2"
    "icon-text-fit": "both",              // <‑‑ key: tells GL to stretch badge
    "icon-text-fit-padding": [1,4,1,4],   // optional but common
    "text-field": "{ref}",                // route number, not a name
    "text-font": ["Open Sans Bold", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"],
    "text-size": 12,
    "text-anchor": "center",              // usually set explicitly
    "symbol-placement": "point"
  }
}
```

* **`icon-image`** – a name that matches the shield‑badge naming convention (`road_*`, `us-interstate_*`, `us-highway_*`, `us-state_*`, etc.) **or** a prefixed name (`my-icons:road_2`).  
* **`icon-text-fit`** – **never** `"none"` for a shield. The presence of `"both"` (or `"width"`/`"height"`) is the *only* way the style tells the renderer to size the badge to the text.  
* **`text-field`** – contains the placeholder `{ref}` (or `{network}_{ref}`) that pulls the route number from the feature. Place‑name layers use `{name}` or `{name_en}` etc.  
* **`text-font`** – usually a font that contains digits (e.g. `"Open Sans Bold"`). Not a strict requirement, but a quick sanity check.  
* **`icon-size`** – often expressed as an expression that depends on `ref_length` (e.g. `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["get", "ref_length"], 1, 0.8, 6, 1.2]`). This is a *secondary* clue.  

If **any** of the above three core clues (`icon-image` matches a shield pattern, `icon-text-fit` ≠ `"none"`, `text-field` contains `{ref}`) are present, you can safely treat the layer as a shield.

---

## 3. A practical, low‑false‑positive heuristic

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a layer looks like a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a style layer object (already parsed JSON)
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Core shield clues – any one of them being true is enough,
  //    but we require at least two to be extra safe.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const clues = [];

  // ---- a) icon‑image matches a known shield naming pattern ----
  if (typeof layout['icon-image'] === 'string') {
    // Strip any sprite‑sheet prefix (e.g. "my-icons:")
    const imgName = layout['icon-image'].split(':').pop();

    // Common patterns used by OSM Bright / Liberty / OpenMapTiles
    const shieldPatterns = [
      /^road_\d+$/,               // generic road badge
      /^us-(interstate|highway|state)_\d+$/, // US specific
      /^default_\d+$/,            // OSM Liberty generic
      /^shield-.*$/               // any custom naming convention you may have
    ];

    if (shieldPatterns.some(p => p.test(imgName))) clues.push('icon-image');
  }

  // ---- b) icon‑text‑fit is not "none" (i.e. badge stretches) ----
  if (layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none') {
    clues.push('icon-text-fit');
  }

  // ---- c) text‑field contains a reference placeholder ----
  if (typeof layout['text-field'] === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(layout['text-field'])) {
    clues.push('text-field');
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Optional sanity checks (they don’t affect the boolean, but
  //    they help you filter out false positives when you log)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // a) text‑anchor is usually set (center/left/right) – not required
  // b) presence of a numeric‑only font (e.g. Open Sans Bold) – optional
  // c) icon‑size expressed as an expression that uses "ref_length" – optional

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Decision: we need at least 2 of the 3 core clues.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  return clues.length >= 2;
}
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it catches | Why it’s shield‑specific |
|-------|----------------|--------------------------|
| **`icon-image` pattern** | Guarantees the layer is trying to draw a badge that exists in a shield sprite sheet. | Place‑name layers usually use generic POI icons (`cafe`, `park`, `city`) that don’t match the shield regexes. |
| **`icon-text-fit` ≠ `"none"`** | Only shields need the badge to stretch to the length of the route number. | Normal point symbols never set `icon-text-fit`; they rely on a fixed‑size icon. |
| **`text-field` contains `{ref}`** | Shields display the route number, not a place name. | Place‑name layers use `{name}` or `{name_en}` etc. |
| **Require ≥ 2 clues** | Prevents a layer that accidentally has a matching icon name (e.g. a custom POI called `road_2`) from being mis‑identified. | Gives a safety net against naming collisions. |

You can tighten or loosen the rule by adjusting the regex list or the “≥ 2 clues” threshold, depending on how many custom shield‑like icons you have in your own sprite sheets.

---

## 4. Edge Cases & How to Handle Them

| Situation | Why the heuristic might mis‑fire | Recommended tweak |
|-----------|----------------------------------|-------------------|
| **Custom “badge” icons that are *not* shields** (e.g. a “badge” for a city‑ranking) | May match `icon-image` pattern (`road_2`) but `text-field` contains `{rank}` instead of `{ref}`. | Keep the “≥ 2 clues” rule – they’ll only have 1 clue (`icon-image`). |
| **Stretchable shields that use a generic badge name** (`shield-badge`) with `icon-text-fit` set, but `text-field` is `{ref}` – works fine. | No problem; the regex list includes `^shield-.*$` as a fallback. |
| **Legacy shield layers that use a zoom‑functioned `symbol-placement`** (e.g. `{stops: [[12, "point"], [14, "line"]]}`) | `symbol-placement` is an expression, not a plain string, so the old heuristic would have missed them. | Our new test **does not look at `symbol-placement` at all**, so it works for both point‑only and point‑plus‑line shields. |
| **Multiple sprite sheets** – the badge lives in a non‑default sheet (`my-icons:us-interstate_2`). | The prefix (`my-icons:`) could be mistaken for a non‑shield name. | We strip everything before the colon before testing the pattern. |
| **SDF shields** – the sprite entry has `"sdf": true`. | The presence of `sdf` does not affect detection; it’s just a rendering hint. | No change needed. |

---

## 5. How to integrate this into a full style‑audit

```js
function auditStyle(style) {
  const shields = [];
  const possibleShields = [];

  for (const layer of style.layers) {
    if (isShieldLayer(layer)) {
      shields.push(layer.id);
    } else if (layer.type === 'symbol' && layer.layout && layer.layout['icon-image']) {
      // Heuristic for “maybe a shield but we’re not sure”
      // (e.g. icon-image matches a pattern but text-field is missing)
      const img = layer.layout['icon-image'].split(':').pop();
      if (/^road_|^us-/.test(img)) possibleShields.push(layer.id);
    }
  }

  console.log('Detected shield layers:', shields);
  console.log('Potential false‑negatives (needs manual check):', possibleShields);
}
```

*The audit now reports three groups:*  

1. **Definite shields** – passed the robust test.  
2. **Potential shields** – have a shield‑like icon name but lack the other clues (maybe a custom badge).  
3. **All other layers** – untouched.

You can then decide whether to flag the “potential” group for manual review or automatically treat them as shields if your workflow tolerates a small false‑positive rate.

---

## 6. Quick checklist for a human reviewer

When you manually inspect a layer that *might* be a shield, verify the following:

| ✅ Should be present | ❌ Should be absent (or irrelevant) |
|----------------------|------------------------------------|
| `icon-image` matches a shield naming convention (or is prefixed with a known sprite sheet) | Generic POI icon names (`cafe`, `park`, `city`) |
| `icon-text-fit` is `"both"` (or `"width"`/`"height"`) | `"none"` (default) |
| `text-field` contains `{ref}` (or `{network}_{ref}`) | `{name}`, `{name_en}`, `{place_name}` |
| `text-anchor` is explicitly set (usually `"center"` for point shields) | Missing *doesn’t* matter, but most shields set it. |
| (optional) `icon-size` expression uses `ref_length` | No reference to `ref_length` |

If **all three core items** are present, you can be 99 % sure the layer is a route shield.

---

## 7. TL;DR Summary for Your

## TL;DR  

*The current test*  

```js
if (layer.type === 'symbol' &&
    layer.layout?.['symbol-placement'] === 'point' &&
    layer.layout?.['text-anchor'] === undefined) {
    // → “shield”
}
```

*is not reliable.*  
`text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when it is omitted, so **any** point‑symbol layer that does not explicitly set the anchor (the overwhelming majority of place‑name / POI layers) will satisfy the test. Real shield layers happen to meet the same condition, but they also have a handful of **additional, shield‑specific properties** that you can use as a much tighter filter.

Below is a short‑form checklist, followed by a concrete “better heuristic” you can drop into your audit script.

---

## 1. Why the old heuristic fails

| Property | What the spec says | What most point‑symbol layers do |
|----------|-------------------|---------------------------------|
| `symbol-placement` | `"point"` for point‑features, `"line"` for line‑features. | **Almost every** point‑symbol layer (place names, POIs, shields) uses `"point"`. |
| `text-anchor` | Optional; defaults to `"center"`. | **Usually omitted**, because `"center"` is the visual default. |
| `text-field` | The string (or expression) that supplies the label text. | Place‑name layers have a literal field name (`{name}` or `{name_en}`); shield layers have `{ref}` (or an expression that builds a ref). |
| `icon-image` | Name of the sprite image that backs the icon. | **Place‑name layers normally have *no* `icon-image`** (they are pure‑text). Shield layers **always** have an `icon-image` that points at a badge sprite (e.g. `"road_2"`, `"us-interstate_3"`, `"shield-badge"`). |
| `icon-text-fit` | `"none"` (default) **or** `"width" / "height" / "both"` for stretchable badges. | Shield layers that use a *stretchable* badge set this to `"both"` (or `"width"`). Pure‑text layers leave it at the default `"none"`. |
| `icon-text-fit-padding` | Array of four numbers – only present when `icon-text-fit` is not `"none"`. | Same as above – only shields have it. |
| `icon-image` **metadata** (`stretchX`, `stretchY`, `content`) | Present only on sprites that are meant to be stretched (the “badge” images). | Shield sprites that are stretchable carry these fields; ordinary POI icons do not. |
| `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-overlap` | Shields often set `icon-allow-overlap: true` (or `"always"`) because the badge must stay visible even when the line label collides. | Place‑name layers usually keep the default (`false`). |

Because **only the first two rows** are used in the current test, you are essentially “flag everything that is a point‑symbol and does not explicitly set an anchor”, which is *exactly* what a plain place‑name layer looks like.

---

## 2. What a shield *actually* looks like in the style spec

A typical shield layer (OSM Bright / OSM Liberty) contains **all** of the following (or a subset that still uniquely identifies a shield):

```json
{
  "id": "road_shield",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "transportation",
  "layout": {
    "symbol-placement": "point",
    "icon-image": ["concat", ["get", "network"], "_", ["to-string", ["get", "ref_length"]]],
    "icon-text-fit": "both",
    "icon-text-fit-padding": [1, 4, 1, 4],
    "text-field": ["get", "ref"],
    "text-font": ["Open Sans Bold", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"],
    "text-size": 12,
    "text-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-allow-overlap": true
  }
}
```

Key take‑aways:

1. **`icon-image` is present** (often built from an expression that uses `network` and `ref_length`).
2. **`icon-text-fit` is *not* `"none"`** – it is `"both"` (or `"width"`).  
   This is the single property that tells the renderer “this icon is a badge that must grow to fit the text”.
3. **`text-field` references the route reference** (`{ref}` or an expression that yields the same).  
   Place‑name layers reference `{name}` or `{name:en}` etc.
4. **`icon-image` points at a sprite that has stretch metadata** (`stretchX`, `stretchY`, `content`).  
   You can verify this by calling `map.getStyle().sprite` → fetch the JSON and look for those fields, or simply rely on the presence of `icon-text-fit`.

If any of those four conditions are missing, you are *probably* not dealing with a shield.

---

## 3. A more robust heuristic

Below is a **drop‑in function** (ESM style) that you can add to your audit script. It returns `true` only when the layer looks like a shield.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer appears to be a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer.
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout ?? {};

  // 2️⃣ Must be placed on points (shields are never line‑placement).
  if (layout['symbol-placement'] !== 'point') return false;

  // 3️⃣ Must have an icon‑image (pure‑text place‑name layers do not).
  if (!('icon-image' in layout)) return false;

  // 4️⃣ The icon must be *text‑fit* (stretchable badge) OR the sprite name
  //    matches a known shield pattern (e.g. "road_", "us-", "shield-").
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  const isTextFit = layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none';
  const looksLikeShieldName =
    typeof iconImage === 'string' &&
    /^(road|us|default|shield|shield-)/i.test(iconImage);

  if (!isTextFit && !looksLikeShieldName) return false;

  // 5️⃣ The text field should be the route reference.
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  const isRefField =
    typeof textField === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(textField) ||
    Array.isArray(textField) && // expression form
    (function containsRef(expr) {
      // naive walk – look for a literal "ref" string in the expression tree
      if (typeof expr === 'string') return expr === 'ref';
      if (Array.isArray(expr)) return expr.some(containsRef);
      return false;
    })(textField);

  if (!isRefField) return false;

  // 6️⃣ (Optional but strong) – the layer usually disables text‑only fallback.
  //    `icon-allow-overlap` is often true for shields.
  //    If you want to be strict, uncomment the next line:
  // if (layout['icon-allow-overlap'] !== true) return false;

  // All checks passed → treat as shield.
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it eliminates |
|-------|--------------------|
| **`type === 'symbol'`** | Non‑symbol layers (background, fill, raster) are ignored. |
| **`symbol-placement === 'point'`** | Shields are never line‑placement; place‑name layers are point‑placement, so we keep them for the next filters. |
| **`icon-image` present** | Pure‑text place‑name layers (the majority of false‑positives) have no `icon-image`. |
| **`icon-text-fit !== 'none'` **or** name matches a shield pattern** | Guarantees the icon is a *badge* that stretches to fit the ref. |
| **`text-field` contains `{ref}`** (or an expression that pulls the `ref` property) | Distinguishes shields (`{ref}`) from place‑names (`{name}`) and other labels. |
| **(optional) `icon-allow-overlap === true`** | Shields usually force the badge to stay visible even when the line label collides; most place‑names keep the default `false`. |

If you want to be *even* stricter, you can fetch the sprite JSON for the sheet(s) referenced by the style and verify that the concrete `icon-image` name you see in the resolved style **actually exists** and carries `stretchX`/`stretchY` metadata. That extra network request is optional for a static audit, but it removes the last edge case where a custom sprite uses a non‑stretchable icon that just happens to be named `road_2`.

---

## 4. Quick sanity‑check against common false‑positives

| Layer type | `symbol-placement` | `text-anchor` | `icon-image` | `icon-text-fit` | `text-field` | **Result** |
|------------|-------------------|--------------|--------------|----------------|--------------|------------|
| **Place‑name (city)** | `"point"` | *omitted* (`undefined`) | **absent** | *default* (`none`) | `"{name}"` | **false** (fails #3) |
| **POI (cafe)** | `"point"` | *omitted* | `"cafe"` | *default* (`none`) | `"{name}"` | **false** (fails #4‑#5) |
| **Shield (US‑I‑5)** | `"point"` | *omitted* | `"us-interstate_2"` | `"both"` | `"{ref}"` | **true** |
| **Shield (generic road)** | `"point"` | *omitted* | `"road_3"` | `"both"` | `"{ref}"` | **true** |
| **Label‑only line‑placement** | `"line"` | *omitted* | **absent** | *default* | `"{ref}"` | **false** (fails #2) |
| **Custom badge (non‑shield)** | `"point"` | *omitted* | `"badge‑star"` | `"both"` | `"{name}"` | **false** (fails #5) |

You can see that the only rows that survive all checks are genuine shields.

---

## 5. Edge cases & how to handle them

| Edge case | Why it can slip through | How to tighten the rule |
|-----------|------------------------|------------------------|
| **Stretchable badge that uses a generic name** (`"badge"` with `icon-text-fit: "both"` but the text field is `{name}`) | The heuristic would think it’s a shield because of `icon-text-fit`. | Add the `text-field` check (step 5). |
| **A custom style that draws a badge for a *different* purpose** (e.g. “rating badge” with `{rating}`) | Same pattern as a shield. | If you only care about *road* shields, also require that the `icon-image` name contains a known network prefix (`road|us|default|shield`). |
| **A shield that uses a *non‑stretchable* pre‑drawn badge** (`icon-text-fit` stays `"none"`). | Some legacy styles ship a separate PNG for each ref length (e.g. `road_1`, `road_2`, …) and never set `icon-text-fit`. | In that case, rely on the **icon‑image naming convention** (`*_1`, `*_2`, …) *and* the presence of a `text-field` that references `{ref}`. The function above already checks for a name that matches `/^(road|us|default|shield)/i`. |
| **A style that omits `icon-image` and uses `icon-rotate` + `text-field` to fake a shield** | Very rare, but possible. | You would need to inspect the sprite JSON for any image that has `stretchX`/`stretchY
